# EF configuration

Map backing fields, owned types, and FKs in `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` under `Domain.Data/Configuration`. Derive table names from the `DbSet` name and the context's naming convention. Do not call `ToTable(...)` in handwritten EF mappings. When changing an existing mapping, check the migration snapshot for an unintended table rename.

Register the configurations from the persistence assembly once in the owning DbContext before calling the base model configuration.

Keys, indexes, ignores, and jsonb conversions. Do not set `HasMaxLength` or `HasColumnType` on strings. Do not set `HasColumnName`.

```csharp
builder.Navigation(e => e.Stages)
    .HasField("_stages")
    .UsePropertyAccessMode(PropertyAccessMode.Field);

builder.HasMany(e => e.Stages)
    .WithOne()
    .HasForeignKey(s => s.PageId);

builder.OwnsOne(e => e.Options, owned =>
{
    owned.Property(x => x.Flag);
});
```

Keep Domain.Data focused on persistence. Express business eligibility, including status-dependent rules, in the domain operation. Avoid embedding eligibility checks such as `HasFilter("status = 0")` in EF mappings. Protect competing writes with a unique key, concurrency token or database lock, and verify the result with concurrent requests. Keep relationship keys and structural unique indexes in the model. Put the first call on the same line as the builder and indent chained calls on subsequent lines. Keep consecutive indexes together.

Use the shared DbContext mapping for ordinary database conflicts. Override it only to translate a specific known constraint into a domain error required by callers. Match the provider error code and constraint, then defer all other errors to the base mapping.

```csharp
public DbSet<Document> Documents { get; set; }
```
